{{Quote|You've grown powerful if you think you can track down Loth-cats with the Force.|Kanan Jarrus to Ezra Bridger|<ref name="Legacy">{{Rebels|Legacy (episode)}}</ref>}}
'''Loth-cats''' were a [[Sentience|non-sentient]] member of the [[feline]] [[tooka]] [[species]] [[Homeworld|native]] to [[Lothal]], often found in the [[grass]]y plains of that [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]]. They were small, temperamental creatures, and while at [[time]]s friendly, were still able hunters with sharp [[Tooth|teeth]] and claws.<ref name="Databank">{{Databank|loth-cat|Loth-cat}}</ref> They were [[Diet|carnivorous]], and were known to feast on [[Loth-rat]]s.<ref name="Alien Archive" />

==Behavior==
{{Quote|Most of Lothal is just seemingly endless grasslands. The most excitement you'll find in these parts is a Loth-cat chasing down a Loth-rat for a quick snack.|Ezra Bridger|<ref name="A New Hero">''[[Star Wars Rebels: A New Hero]]''</ref>}}
[[File:LothCat-TVE.png|thumb|left|200px|An angered Loth-cat]]
Loth-cats were [[Sentience|non-sentient]],<ref name="Helmet Collection 36" /> [[Diet|carnivorous]] felines, preying mostly on loth-rats and other small herbivores of Lothal's plains.<ref name="Bestiary">''[[Star Wars Bestiary, Vol. 1: Creatures of the Galaxy]]''</ref> Although, they would also eat [[insect]]s and [[bird]]s.<ref name="Epic Battles" /> Loth-cats were very persistent hunters and would stalk their prey for [[Standard hour|hours]], or even [[Standard day|days]]. Loth-cats led solitary lives, coming together only to mate and raise kits.<ref name="Bestiary" /> Loth-cats, when cornered, were very dangerous, and would fight against their would-be predators, most of the time going for the face, until they escaped or the aggressor backed off. They wouldn't typically attack creatures larger than themselves. Although it was very difficult, loth-cats could be domesticated, and they made good, albeit destructive, pets. With enough time and patience, they could even be taught to hunt and retrieve for their owners.<ref name="NoP" />

==History==
{{Quote|You're resisting. He can sense it.''"<br />"''He can sense it? What is he, like a Padawan cat? Oh!''"<br />"''You don't seem to be getting this.''"<br />"''I get that this furball's trying to kill me. Give me your lightsaber and I'll make the connection.''"<br />"''Excuse me?|Kanan Jarrus and Ezra Bridger, as Ezra tries to tame a Loth-cat|<ref name="Empire Day">{{Rebels|Empire Day (episode)}}</ref>}}
[[File:Loth-cat_ToL.png|thumb|right|220px|A trio of Loth-cats in the fields of Lothal]]
Loth-[[cat]]s could be found in the grasslands on the planet Lothal where they used the [[grass]] as cover while hunting [[Diet|herbivores]].<ref name="VE">''[[Star Wars: The Visual Encyclopedia]]''</ref> Loth-cats markings were camouflaged in Lothal's tall grass.<ref name="Epic Battles" /> One Loth-cat was once surprised by a drink carton falling on its head, seemingly from nowhere. In truth, the carton had fallen from the [[Spectres|rebel]] [[Starship|ship]] ''[[Ghost]]'', flying high above the grasslands.<ref name="Rise of the Old Masters">{{Rebels|Rise of the Old Masters}}</ref>

On another occasion, [[Kanan Jarrus]] attempted to [[Jedi training|teach]] his [[Padawan]] learner, [[Ezra Bridger]], to feel the [[living Force]] through connection with a Loth-cat. Ezra failed the exercise, however, due to his distraction and irritation at the fact that it was [[Empire Day]], and his own fifteenth [[birthday]]. The Loth-cat instead attacked Bridger.<ref name="Empire Day" />

[[File:Sleepy_Loth-cat.jpg|thumb|left|240px|A sleeping Loth-cat]]
Some [[time]] later, while [[scout]]ing out an [[Imperial Communications Center]], Bridger demonstrated mastery of the same trick in causing a Loth-cat to attack an [[Galactic Empire|Imperial]] [[dwarf probe droid]] as a distraction, allowing himself and his companions to escape.<ref name="Call to Action">{{Rebels|Call to Action}}</ref>

Some time later, two Loth-cats were surprised in the grasslands by the [[R4 courier droid]] [[264]] falling from the sky. The droid was not destroyed, and righted itself, finding itself in curious company.<ref name="Rebel Resolve">{{Rebels|Rebel Resolve}}</ref>

Bridger first saw a rare [[white Loth-cat]] in a [[Force vision|vision]], and it led him to the conclusion that by following it, he would be able to find clues about his [[parent]]s' fate.<ref name="Legacy">{{Rebels|Legacy (episode)}}</ref> Later Bridger and [[Sabine Wren]] [[Theft of the TIE Defender Elite|attempted]] to gather information about an [[TIE/D Defender Elite]], only to have the [[TIE series|TIE's]] [[Stormtrooper|guards]] attacked by a group of pair of loth-cats. A white cat also appeared with a [[loth-wolf]].<ref name="Flight of the Defender">{{Rebels|Flight of the Defender}}</ref>

==Loth-cats in the galaxy==
[[File:Loth-cat_The_Mandalorian.png|thumb|right|250px|A Loth-cat on Sorgan]]
Around [[9 ABY]],<ref name="TMDate">
{{MandoDate|12}}</ref> a Loth-cat resided within a [[Sorgan Common House|common house]] on the [[planet]] [[Sorgan]]. It snarled at [[Din Grogu|Grogu]] from under a table as he passed by.<ref name="Chapter 4">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 4: Sanctuary}}</ref> During the same year,<ref name="AhsokaDate">{{AhsokaSeriesDate}}</ref> a Loth-cat resided in the [[LothalNet comm tower E-272]], where it lived together with Sabine Wren,<ref name="Part One">{{Ahsoka|Part One: Master and Apprentice}}</ref> who named it [[Murley]].<ref name="MurleyDB">{{Databank|murley|Murley}}</ref>

Around [[34 ABY]],<ref name="GEDate">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of [[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge|''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge]] to [[34 ABY]].</ref> Loth-cats were imported to the planet [[Batuu]] and sold as pets in the [[Creature Stall]] at [[Black Spire Outpost]].<ref name="GE">{{GEAttr|Creature Stall}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
Loth-cats were created for the [[canon]] animated series ''[[Star Wars Rebels]]'', first appearing in the fifth episode of the [[Star Wars Rebels Season One|first season]], "[[Rise of the Old Masters]]",<ref name="Rise of the Old Masters" /> released in [[October]] [[2014]].<ref name="Insider 156">{{InsiderCite|156|Star Wars Rebels Recounted|''Star Wars Rebels'' Recounted}}</ref>

They later also appeared in the Creature Stall gift shop at the [[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge|''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge]] theme land<ref name="GE" /> and made live-action appearances in the television series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]''<ref name="Chapter 4" /> and the [[Ahsoka Season 1|first season]] of ''[[Star Wars: Ahsoka]]''.<ref name="Part One" /> During a panel at [[Celebration Japan (2025)|Celebration Japan]] on [[April 20]], [[2025]], ''Rebels'' and ''Ahsoka'' creator [[Dave Filoni]] confirmed that Murley would return for [[Ahsoka Season 2|''Ahsoka'' Season 2]] and also revealed the introduction of Loth kitten. Filoni explained that these young Loth-cats would, as they were not yet able to walk, roll around instead. [[Ahsoka Tano]] actor [[Rosario Dawson]] also described them as "little Loth balls".<ref name="Ahsoka-5-Things">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-celebration-2025-ahsoka-season-2|text=5 Things We Learned at the ''Star Wars'' Celebration Japan ''Ahsoka'' Panel}}</ref>

The ''[[Nexus of Power]]'' sourcebook for [[Fantasy Flight Games]]' ''[[Star Wars: Force and Destiny]]'' [[roleplaying game]]<ref name="NoP" /> as well as ''[[Star Wars Bestiary, Vol. 1: Creatures of the Galaxy]]'' say the Loth-cats lived solitary lives, coming together only to mate and raise kits.<ref name="Bestiary" /> Meanwhile, the reference book ''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]'' says the Loth-cats lived in family groups,<ref name="SWECG" /> while ''[[Star Wars Rebels: Visual Guide: Epic Battles]]'' states both parents raised their litters of kittens together in underground dens, protecting them from [[Sabercat]]s and Loth-wolves.<ref name="Epic Battles" />

